# Worked traces are bounded to the brief; these are prose, not new tool rules.
walkthroughs={
0: "Work through a supplied change request for photo 12. First ask whether the client wants the collection or one identified resource: this case concerns one photo. Then ask which operation is intended: changing it selects PUT/PATCH at /photos/{photo}, not POST at the collection URL. Finally, copy the specified success and non-success statuses into the contract. Repeat that reasoning for a new-photo request and the answer changes to POST at /photos. Writing those decisions in separate columns makes it possible to check method, URL and outcome independently. Do not choose an exact success status just from the fact that success belongs to 200–299; the supplied endpoint contract must state which success outcome is expected.",
2: "For a second trace, let key contain ‘subtitle’. If subtitle was explicitly assigned null, record[key] reads null. If that property was never assigned, the same lookup returns undefined. Assigning a value to that absent property creates it. Now distinguish this property assignment from replacing record itself: a const binding permits the former object change but prevents the latter binding reassignment. In supplied data-handling code, annotate each assignment with what it targets. Does it target the binding, a named property, or a nested object's property? That question keeps the const rule and the absent-property rule from being blended into a vague claim that the entire record is fixed.",
4: "Review the example as five assignment-and-inspection pairs rather than one undifferentiated record. Quantity is a whole number, price is a floating-point number, title is text, published is a boolean, and subtitle is null. Then change only the quantity assignment to quoted text. The variable name is unchanged, but the value and its runtime type are different. Use the paired var_dump inspections to make that contrast explicit. This gives you a method for tracing supplied PHP values: identify the most recent assignment, inspect its value and type, and avoid inferring type solely from the name chosen for the variable. A name such as quantity does not replace inspection of the value it currently holds.",
5: "Check two supplied integration requirements side by side. Requirement A identifies the product's own browser SPA and API under a shared top-level domain. Record ‘cookie-based Laravel session’ as the choice. Requirement B identifies a third-party client; record ‘bearer API token in Authorization’. The distinction is the chosen client model and its stated scope, not whether the API exposes resources. This decision does not claim to complete configuration, CSRF setup or protected-route enforcement. It simply tells later work which authentication model must be implemented. If a requirement does not meet the stated first-party shared-domain case, do not quietly reuse that case's choice without resolving the requirement.",
6: "Take the supplied nested markup and mark each ordinary opening tag with its matching closing tag. The innermost open element should close first. An overlap is visible when an outer closing tag appears while an inner ordinary element is still open. Next, inspect the opening tags alone for attributes: their name=\"value\" pairs belong there, separated by spaces, not in the element's content. Finally, isolate void elements such as img so they are not incorrectly put through the ordinary wrapped-content pattern. This review separates three checks that are easy to confuse when scanning markup quickly: ordinary pairing, legal nesting and attribute placement. It is a structural trace of supplied elements, not an assumption that all HTML elements have closing tags.",
7: "Use three supplied inputs to test the decision. With numeric 200, the strict status === 200 predicate matches. With text '200', that same strict comparison does not convert the value to make it match. With another numeric status, follow the next else-if predicate, and if no earlier predicate matches, follow else. For a compound boolean predicate, separately record each boolean component before applying &&, || or !. This makes it clear whether an unexpected branch comes from a type mismatch, a particular component predicate or the order of alternatives. Keep the selected body's behavior separate from the predicate that selected it: the conditional determines which body runs, while the exercise supplies what that body does.",
8: "In a supplied callback trace, label three sets of names: the enclosing function's locals, the nested helper's parameters and the helper's own locals. A helper read can then be located in one of those sets. If it reads the enclosing title, that is access to enclosing scope, not evidence that title is global. If it creates a helper-only local, that local remains inside the helper's scope. Finally follow the return at each boundary: the outer function may return a function value, and the helper may later return a result when invoked. This distinction explains why the arguments supplied to the eventual callback and the local state it can already access are different sources of values.",
9: "For a supplied controller-generation task, keep a short evidence trail. The list output establishes that the command is available; help establishes the argument and option form; the make:controller invocation requests the named artifact. If the requested artifact name changes from PhotoController to another supplied name, change that argument, not the command's purpose. If the development task itself changes, go back to command discovery and help. This prevents memorizing one invocation as a universal project operation. The completed trace ends at controller artifact generation. It does not infer routes, database work or public endpoint responses from a file being generated, because those are not outcomes this command-selection concept teaches.",
10: "For a nested record, trace the outer key first and then the inner key. If owner selects a nested array and name selects an entry inside it, the second bracket access applies to that inner array. For result construction, inspect whether the left side has a named key or empty brackets. A named title key can be inserted or replaced; empty brackets append another value. These differences can be checked on a tiny supplied example before following a larger handler. Draw the array before and after each assignment, preserving the distinction between a record's named fields and a list of records. This stays within PHP arrays rather than assuming an object-property syntax for array access.",
11: "Test the supplied numeric boundary at the value immediately on the boundary as well as a value on either side. At a maximum of 5, <= 5 includes 5 and < 5 does not. Next, test the grouped boolean decision with one true and one false component: && requires both, whereas || requires at least one. Separately inspect strict comparisons using equal-looking values of different types. These three traces answer different questions: whether the boundary is inclusive, whether the eligibility rule combines requirements or alternatives, and whether values and types both match. Parentheses make the intended boolean grouping explicit, so read that grouping before assigning the combined predicate its result.",
12: "Check the caption connection in both files. In the HTML, the value of class is caption. In the stylesheet, the selector begins .caption. If you see Caption in one place and caption in the other, correct the mismatch rather than guessing that an unrelated declaration is the cause. Once the names match, inspect the external link to styles.css, save and refresh to check the supplied styling. Each observation answers a separate question: which elements the class selector matches, whether the declarations are written in a rule, and whether the page is attached to the external stylesheet. Keep the desired property values supplied by the exercise; the trace is about correctly applying those declarations.",
14: "Track source and result explicitly for the two-record array. Mapping titles yields two returned title values; filtering by one title retains the corresponding record element. Now inspect a callback that writes into a received record object. That write concerns the source object even if map returns a newly built array. To reason about nonmutating derivation, check both whether the array result is new and whether callbacks avoid source-object writes. A source array that still has its original identity is not enough evidence that all its records are unchanged. This is why the same example should be traced at the collection level and at the record-object level, not just by counting the length of the returned array.",
15: "Draw the fulfilled and rejected paths for the supplied dependency. In the fulfilled path, the awaited record becomes available and only then does the title-dependent step proceed. In the rejected path, control reaches the matching catch or propagates failure to the caller; the success-dependent result is not available. Finally look one boundary outward: does the enclosing helper return or await the dependent work? If it merely starts the work, its own completion no longer represents completion of that dependency. This outer check is as important as seeing await inside a nested operation. The aim is a complete chain in which each caller can track the work it depends on, not isolated use of asynchronous keywords.",
19: "Give the same chain three inputs: one matching if, one matching only an elseif, and one matching none of them. Mark the selected body for each input and compare it with the supplied outcome requirement. The first true alternative determines the selected branch; else supplies the remaining case. If two supplied conditions could both be true, order matters in the chain because earlier matching alternatives prevent later alternatives from being selected. Review that order against the requirement rather than treating each branch as an independent test. This walkthrough is about selecting the body, so it does not introduce unrelated operations inside the branch.",
20: "Trace the function contract from the calling file through the result. First list the declared parameter types and whether a nullable declaration permits null. Next note whether the calling file enables strict scalar typing or uses default scalar coercion. Preserve the stated acceptance of int for a float declaration under strict scalar typing. Then follow return: it sends the result to the caller and ends the function. An untyped no-return function yields null, which is not a reason to assume an int return contract can omit its result. Keeping the input boundary and return boundary separate helps identify which part of a supplied function contract a value must satisfy.",
21: "Compare daily and every-minute versions of the same supplied task. The callback names the work in both cases; changing the chained frequency changes its declared due times. schedule:list lets you inspect those next times rather than infer them from the task's name. A daily declaration here means daily at midnight. The walkthrough ends with that schedule inspection, not with a claim that a server process has already evaluated and executed it.",
23: "Trace two object creations with distinct supplied constructor inputs. Mark which property initialization belongs to each instance. When an instance method accesses $this->property, use the state of the object on which the method was invoked, not whichever constructor call appeared most recently in the file. Then follow $object->method(...) from the external variable to the target instance's behavior. This separates the class as a definition from each object as an instance with initialized state. The exercise can supply the method's body; the important trace here is how constructor arguments, properties, $this and instance method invocation connect without treating all instances as one shared object.",
24: "Write the accumulator's values alongside each visit: initial 0, then 2 after the first value, then 5 after the second. If it were initialized again in each loop body, it would not carry the intended summary across visits. For a keyed result, write the current key beside the current value and use the key-and-value foreach form. Finally distinguish changing the loop variable from reference-based source editing: the ordinary value form is not a reference to the original element.",
26: "Read the supplied .vue file by role. Find the script setup section and its top-level bindings, then locate the template's use of one of those bindings. Inspect the optional style block separately as CSS. If a title is not available where expected, start by checking whether its declaration belongs to the stated top-level script-setup binding pattern. This review does not require learning every directive at once: it establishes the structure into which those later bindings and interactions fit.",
27: "Compare a read and create request built by the same wrapper. The read uses GET and no body. The create sets its method, JSON content type and serialized payload body. Both use the shared request interface and return asynchronous work to the caller. For the expected JSON response, follow the Response into awaited response.json and then the returned parsed data. These are distinct values at distinct stages; the serialized outgoing body is not the parsed incoming result. This concept traces the intended successful JSON path, leaving status and rejection handling to their explicit request-failure boundary.",
29: "For the same route, draw an accepted and rejected request. Both enter Laravel's request-handling process, but only the accepted one passes assigned middleware and reaches the handler. Follow its returned response through the middleware chain and back to the browser. The rejected request stops at the request-check boundary instead. This comparison gives meaning to the lifecycle stages: handler-side evidence belongs to a path that reached the handler, while an earlier rejection can still produce a browser response. When locating a supplied failed trace, mark the last stage supported by evidence rather than assuming a particular error must have arisen in the controller.",
31: "Review the supplied migration in two directions. In up, identify the resulting table and columns and the foreign key reference. In down, identify the operations that reverse that change. After application with migrate, the relevant outstanding migrations have been applied. Before rollback, inspect which files belong to the latest batch, since that command may reverse several files. Do not read an individual file's down method as proof that the command will affect that file alone. This keeps schema-change reasoning separate from batch-level operation and keeps the documented integrity constraint distinct from simply giving a field the name user_id.",
32: "Trace a matching failure, a nonmatching failure and normal completion through the same try/catch boundary. A matching catch handles its throwable; a nonmatching throwable continues outward, with finally still running as control leaves. For return behavior, remember the brief's additional warning: a return from finally can override an earlier return from try or catch. Therefore, ‘finally runs’ is not a promise that it merely performs cleanup while preserving an earlier returned result. Keep that warning separate from the propagation trace. It explains why a finally body must be read, not mentally skipped as automatically harmless cleanup.",
33: "Compare two supplied implementing classes against the same interface. For each required public method, check that the class provides a compatible signature. Then inspect the caller's dependency type: it should express the interface so either conforming implementation can be substituted without rewriting that dependency. This trace distinguishes shared contract from interchangeable names; compatibility is established by the declared implementation and methods, not merely by two classes performing similar-looking work.",
35: "Record the startup command and printed development URL for the supplied project. Open that actual URL and check an edit to a supplied module through Vite's hot module replacement behavior. The entrypoint is root index.html, and the observed module update belongs to development. This is not evidence that a production bundle has been generated or hosted. Keep those later outcomes outside the development-server check.",
36: "Pair the supplied listener registration and removal visibly. Mark the listener registered after initial rendering in onMounted, then find removal of that same listener in onUnmounted. Also locate hook registration during synchronous setup, so the component association is explicit. The trace follows one side effect through its component lifetime rather than assuming cleanup from the presence of any unmount hook.",
37: "Track a title update under both representations. With a ref, the script assignment targets title.value and the top-level template reads its unwrapped value. With a reactive record, the script targets a property on the proxy. Now compare a local primitive value taken by destructuring: assigning or reading that local does not preserve the original property connection. Likewise, replacing the reactive object breaks the original connection. Annotate the target of each supplied assignment before deciding whether it follows the reactive representation's rules. A value looking identical immediately after creation does not establish that it will remain connected during later changes.",
38: "Trace a supplied /photos destination from its route record to the matched component displayed through RouterView. Verify app.use(router) occurs before mount. Then compare a RouterLink click to router.push of that destination: one expresses a declarative navigation link and the other performs programmatic navigation. Both depend on the configured destination mapping. This trace concerns client navigation, so direct nested URL hosting is not inferred from it.",
39: "For a supplied 422 case, write down ‘Response received’ before reading the field-error body. That outcome belongs to HTTP-response handling, not to the path where the fetch operation rejected. For a transport or parsing rejection, write down the try/catch boundary instead. An ok check identifies 200–299 success before treating a body as successful data. This separation makes the caller's choices explicit even if several cases ultimately display error feedback. Similar screen appearance does not make a received HTTP status and a rejected asynchronous operation the same event.",
41: "Trace an existing record update in order. Find retrieves by primary key; fill applies permitted mass-assignment input to the instance; save persists the changes. The allowlist should contain the editable title but not the protected ownership field. Now compare a create operation, which inserts and returns a model, and a delete operation, which removes its stored record. These are different persistence operations, not variations of one generic assignment. Check the submitted attribute set against $fillable before following either create or fill so an unexpected protected field is not accidentally made assignable.",
42: "Put the supplied contract beside route:list. Compare method first, public URI second and controller action third. A route declared as /user inside routes/api.php has /api/user as its default public address. That prefix transformation belongs in the comparison. If the method or path is wrong, do not infer that changing the controller's response will make the request target the right registered operation.",
43: "Compare dispatch to the supplied asynchronous connection with dispatch using sync. In the asynchronous case, verify that work is left pending for later execution outside the request. In the sync case, execution is immediate. Both pass input into the job's constructor and use handle for its work, so class structure alone does not distinguish their timing. The connection and the observed pending-work outcome supply that distinction.",
45: "Run the total mentally on a tiny selected set. Start the local total at 0, add 4, then add 6 and return 10. A filter that excludes the second record changes which amounts contribute; it is not a write into the source amounts. The getter reads reactive dependencies and produces a cached derived result until those dependencies change. Review any supplied getter by separating its selection, accumulator updates and returned result from prohibited source-state writes or asynchronous requests.",
46: "Trace two calls to the use-prefixed function separately. Each call creates its own internal refs when the composable is structured that way, and each returns the actions operating on that call's state. A shared definition does not merge those refs into one shared instance. This lets the learner identify behavior reuse without assuming cross-component state sharing.",
47: "Check four output cases separately: interpolated text, an ordinary dynamic attribute, a null or undefined attribute value, and a boolean disabled attribute. The first uses mustaches; the second uses v-bind or its colon shorthand; the third removes the attribute; the fourth follows the stated truthy-or-empty-string presence rule. Do not transfer one case's rule to another merely because all values come from component data.",
48: "Trace the documented boolean access for stored 0 and stored 1, then add the null case, which is not cast. For a date field, identify application-facing date objects separately from conversion of supported assigned values for storage. Finally distinguish the serialization format from database storage format. These checks prevent the word ‘cast’ from becoming an unsupported promise that every representation and null case behaves identically.",
49: "Use three supplied records to evaluate each chained where predicate. Keep only records satisfying all ordinary where clauses, then apply the chosen ordering and retrieve with get. Writing the surviving set before sorting makes clear which change belongs to filtering and which belongs to order. Ascending is the default, while descending must be selected explicitly in this syntax.",
50: "Follow the dataset from its seeder run method into DatabaseSeeder's call list and the selected command. That shows which intended inserts belong to the seed operation. Separately mark migrate:fresh --seed as dropping tables before migration and seeding. The ordinary db:seed operation and the destructive rebuild should be chosen according to the supplied development task, not because either command includes the word seed.",
51: "Test the title requirement with a missing title, a nonstring title and a string beyond the supplied character limit. Required, string and max address those different invalid cases. A nullable optional field allows null; that permission is separate from whether a field is required. Trace each rejected JSON payload to its 422 message and errors mapping before controller action execution. This makes the validation boundary observable and keeps a validation-only request's authorize choice tied to the fact that authorization is handled elsewhere.",
53: "Place the protected handler after the middleware's decision in a trace. The continuing branch calls $next($request); the rejection branch returns its own response. If the middleware is assigned to a group, repeat that boundary for every route inside it rather than treating only the first route as protected. The diagram should show exactly why a rejected request never reaches the later handler.",
54: "Compare make and create with the same supplied overrides. Make gives a model instance without persistence; create stores the generated model. Count with create adds the requested number of persisted records. The override set determines attributes needed for the case, while definition supplies defaults. Keep instance creation, record persistence and case-specific values visible as separate checks.",
55: "For a deployment change, identify which cached material is relevant before choosing a command. Route changes require rebuilding the route cache. Cached configuration changes the stated environment-loading behavior outside config files. optimize covers configuration, events, routes and views; optimize:clear also affects keys in the default cache driver. That last effect belongs in the operational decision rather than being hidden behind the assumption that every clearing command touches only one optimization file.",
57: "Put the two supplied writes inside one callback and mark its completion point. On normal completion the transaction commits; if a write throws, the database changes roll back and the exception goes outward again. In a second trace, move the first write outside the callback and observe why that placement no longer expresses the required shared transaction boundary. The placement is the central lesson, not merely the presence of DB::transaction somewhere nearby.",
58: "Compare the users route segment with the action parameter character for character: {user} pairs with $user of type User. Then identify the lookup column: the documented example uses the primary key, while {post:slug} chooses slug. A missing match yields 404. These three checks separate name matching, lookup choice and missing-record behavior.",
59: "Draw the requested relative path and then the forwarded path. Mark the matching proxy prefix, backend target and any rewrite. Compare the forwarded method and URL with route:list. This reveals whether the prefix is removed when it should be retained or retained when it should be removed. A configured target does not by itself prove that the transformed request matches Laravel's route.",
60: "Record build output and local preview separately. The supplied build script produces the production bundle from index.html into default dist. The supplied preview script serves that dist locally for checking. If preview passes, record it as local built-output verification, not as proof that production hosting is configured. This preserves the intended operational boundary.",
61: "Trace registration and one later source change. Without immediate, the watch callback is lazy; with immediate it also runs at registration. For one reactive property, the getter keeps the source tied to that property rather than handing watch its current primitive value. Place the intended persistence in the callback and keep the source narrow. Finally identify component-bound stopping or the explicit returned stop function as the stated watcher lifetime controls.",
62: "Mark the user action, handler invocation and ref update in order. A named DOM handler receives the native event and performs script-side .value updates as needed. A small inline template expression can instead make the documented count increment. The binding is the connection from the event to behavior; the tracked update is the connection from behavior to component state.",
63: "Trace a parent's changed title through a dynamic prop binding into the child. Compare that with a child-local value initialized from the initial prop: the local copy does not track later parent changes. For an object prop, inspect nested writes separately because nested references remain exposed. The readonly input rule and the caution about nested parent-data mutation both matter to ownership.",
64: "For an initially false flag, v-if has not created its branch, whereas v-show keeps the element and hides it by display. Toggle the flag and compare the two mechanisms without treating them as identical because they can look similar on screen. Then inspect adjacency of the else alternatives. The stated initial and toggle cost differences support the mechanism distinction, not a universal rule that one directive is always preferable.",
65: "Imagine the Harbour row has temporary input before the records reorder. Label that input with Harbour's stable id, not just the row position. After the reorder, identity keys let Vue reuse nodes by item identity; default in-place patching can leave temporary state with the wrong record. This trace explains why primitive stable unique identifiers matter beyond merely satisfying a syntax requirement.",
66: "Use one supplied text value that contains markup-like characters. Under interpolation, it remains escaped text. Under v-html, raw HTML is inserted, creating the stated exposure for untrusted content. Using untrusted content as a component template crosses an even different boundary, permitting arbitrary JavaScript execution. Compare those roles directly rather than assuming that all ways of putting content on screen treat it as data.",
68: "Draw one post and two comments with matching post_id values, then another post with a different id. Reading the first post's comments follows the child foreign keys back to that post. Reading either comment's post resolves its matching parent. The hasMany and belongsTo methods describe opposite directions of this same foreign-key association, not unrelated relationships inferred from similar model names.",
69: "Split each supplied query into trusted structure and submitted value. Where receives the search value separately, and whereRaw uses a placeholder with a separate binding when raw SQL is necessary. A requested column cannot be handled by that same value-binding mechanism, so check its trusted origin independently. This trace keeps the protection claim bounded: parameter binding helps protect against SQL injection, while raw expressions and arbitrary column choices still require care.",
70: "Follow the active scope's single reusable constraint into a caller that adds another where before get. The scope modifies the builder with active = 1; the caller remains able to combine that constraint with its own requirement. The Laravel 12 Scope attribute and compatible Builder signature establish the declared form, while the absence of retrieval inside the scope preserves the constraint-composition pattern.",
71: "Trace one allowed title from model to resource array to outer JSON response. Then check an internal field that is not on the public output list: explicit output selection should not expose it automatically. Finally inspect default data wrapping. Field selection and wrapping describe different levels of the response, and the consumer's contract must account for both rather than checking only the title's presence.",
72: "Match each registered API action to its operation, then follow store's actual work. Accepted attributes go into Model::create; the specified public body and creation status go into response()->json. The omitted create and edit routes are HTML-form routes, not evidence that the API has no creation or update operations. Keeping action names and operations paired resolves that naming distinction.",
73: "Compare an update omitting subtitle with one explicitly providing null. Sometimes limits validation to the present optional field, while nullable permits the explicit null. In validated data, array_key_exists distinguishes the present-null key from omission; isset does not make that same distinction. Fill only present validated attributes and save them. That trace preserves the supplied optional-field semantics without adding a default that the client did not send.",
74: "Follow one captured request through a method-and-URL comparison, received-status or rejection classification and matching log context. If a diagnostic change adds safe context, repeat the same request and inspect the new evidence. Each step should refine the reached boundary. The approach does not claim that every screen error has a fixed cause; it establishes what the observed request and server evidence actually support.",
75: "Check the origin's host and port against the stateful list, the shared-domain requirement against the supplied deployment and statefulApi in the middleware configuration. Then inspect the Accept and Origin or Referer evidence on the browser request. These requirements together define the first-party SPA request boundary. A correct domain list alone should not be treated as completing every other configured requirement.",
76: "Trace one request through pending, fulfilled-empty and fulfilled-populated outcomes, then another through failure. Loading begins before await and resets in finally; data and error reflect the outcome. In the successful list branch, zero items selects empty feedback instead of continued loading. This makes an empty result a meaningful successful outcome and prevents the same blank state from standing for both waiting and completion.",
77: "Mark the direct child-parent boundary for the supplied event. The child emits its named event and payload; the parent listens and updates the state it owns. Keep that path separate from a DOM event's bubbling model. If an ancestor is not the direct parent listener, the event is not automatically propagated to it. The child communicates a requested change while respecting readonly parent-owned input.",
78: "Change the title field in the supplied form, then follow v-model's synchronized JavaScript state into the submit handler's JSON payload. The form's prevented default navigation keeps the submission in the component handler, but it does not automatically choose the correct payload. Compare the serialized form values with unrelated component state so the request represents the user's current fields.",
79: "Run the same protected nested URL through in-app navigation and a fresh direct request. The first uses the running client router; the second reaches the host before Vue Router starts. Reload tests that host path too. A correct fallback preserves existing files before serving index.html for client routes. Unknown paths also reach the SPA under a catch-all, so the client not-found route remains a separate configured outcome.",
80: "For id 12 and then id 18, write the value supplied to the watch callback's loading operation. The component instance is reused for the same matched route component, so the changed parameter must drive new loading. With immediate the same watcher handles initial id 12 as well. Keep the identifier getter narrow instead of assuming another lifecycle invocation will necessarily reload the detail.",
82: "Trace the public sort option through Rule::in validation and its trusted mapping before orderBy. Separately trace a filter's submitted value into a bound where value. Validation rejects unsupported choices, and mapping establishes the permitted query structure. These are not redundant steps: bound values do not bind columns, and a public option need not simply be copied as an arbitrary column name.",
84: "Count the documented books-and-authors pattern rather than guessing from rendered output. Lazy per-book author access can add a query per book; with('author') retrieves the needed authors together in the documented two-query example. If a related select omits required identifiers or foreign keys, displayed columns alone are insufficient. Keep both query pattern and identifying columns in the review.",
85: "Write the pivot membership set before each operation. Attach adds an association and detach removes it without deleting the models. Sync makes the set match the supplied ids, including removal of omitted ids; syncWithoutDetaching retains those omitted memberships. A before-and-after identifier set makes this removal distinction clearer than saying every method merely ‘updates relationships’.",
86: "Pair each supplied assertion with one contract requirement. Status checks the HTTP outcome, a JSON fragment checks required data presence, a JSON path checks one field and validation-error assertions check field feedback. The invalid case combines 422 with the field mapping. Read that test as a set of explicit public checks, not a generic claim that an API call passed.",
88: "Trace accepted credentials and rejected credentials separately through Auth::attempt. The submitted plaintext candidate is checked against the stored hash, and the boolean result selects the login response. Building the JSON body and status remains an explicit contract step after that check. This trace deliberately stops short of claiming to finish every session-security operation, keeping the authentication decision distinct from later session defense.",
89: "Follow the cookie names and request order visibly. CSRF initialization sets XSRF-TOKEN; the decoded value supplies X-XSRF-TOKEN when the library does not do so; successful login establishes the session cookie. These are distinct parts of the chosen flow. A later 401 or 419 may call for login recovery, not disabling CSRF. Do not collapse initialization, credential submission and subsequent authenticated requests into one unexplained action.",
90: "Separate newly issued plaintext from stored token hash in the supplied token record. Deliver plainTextToken immediately and use it in the client's bearer header. For revocation, write the selected token id and confirm the deletion scope: the selected where deletion leaves other tokens, while deleting the whole tokens relationship revokes all. The scope of removal is a concrete decision, not just whether some token method ran.",
91: "Trace a public form interaction in test order. Mount with props, await input entry, await submission and then assert the emitted payload or visible text. These assertions describe what a consumer receives or sees. A private internal variable having the expected value does not replace that public trace, nor should an assertion skip the awaited user-interaction boundary.",
92: "Follow one returned field name into its reactive errors entry and then its nearby rendered messages. Before the next request, clear stale error state; on a new 422, replace the mapping. This distinguishes current server feedback from a message left behind by an earlier submission. The 422 response still needs status inspection and parsing rather than relying on fetch to reject it.",
93: "Label each supplied value by its consumers. If both list and summary need the confirmed title, they need one shared source. If only the edit form needs an unconfirmed draft, it can stay component-owned. This ownership trace prevents independent copies of shared values from becoming competing sources while also avoiding a store full of values no other component uses.",
94: "Compare three allowed output cases from the supplied contract: identifier-only, included loaded collection and omitted unloaded collection. Explicit toArray output selects public related fields; whenLoaded determines omission when the collection was not loaded. With at the parent query boundary coordinates the included case. Do not treat omission and an included empty collection as automatically the same shape for the consumer.",
96: "Keep the server logout sequence and visible SPA change in separate columns. Auth::logout removes authenticated user information; invalidate flushes data and replaces the identifier; regenerateToken refreshes the CSRF token. AssertGuest checks the unauthenticated result. A navigation label can be updated too, but it is not evidence that those session operations occurred.",
97: "Trace two consumers from the exported useStore call to the same property. Both should read through the store or through refs obtained with storeToRefs when destructuring state or getters. Direct destructuring breaks that reactive connection. Also confirm Pinia is installed before mount. This connects store definition, application installation and reactive consumption rather than treating any one as the whole setup.",
98: "Check the supplied mutation request in two parts: session credential inclusion and the CSRF header. Credentials include carries cookies for the configured cross-origin session API, while the decoded XSRF-TOKEN value supplies X-XSRF-TOKEN. Fetch does not do that copy automatically. Then verify Accept and Origin or Referer evidence for the first-party boundary. None of these checks requires replacing the chosen session model with a bearer token.",
99: "Compare anonymous JSON access with a stateful authenticated SPA request. Auth:sanctum enforces the boundary, and assertUnauthorized verifies the anonymous 401 case. In the accepted protected route, request->user retrieves the authenticated identity. Applying middleware to a group covers every route inside it. The trace establishes authentication, not a model-specific permission decision merely inferred from a signed-in user.",
100: "Observe the same mocked request at two times. Before flushPromises, assert the loading indicator. After awaiting flushPromises, assert the configured fulfilled result or rejected-error outcome. Reset mocks between cases and set each response anew. This prevents a success result or call history from one test from silently providing the next case's setup and prevents the loading observation from being skipped.",
101: "Compare approved and disallowed origins using the required preflight method and headers. The approved credentialed response needs its specific origin and credentials permission; the disallowed origin should not receive access permission. Publishing and editing configuration are setup actions, while the response headers and preflight observations verify the resulting boundary. A wildcard value is not the approved credentialed result.",
102: "Trace the named ability for one permitted user and one denied user. The gate callback supplies the role or capability decision; authorize enforces it before work and returns 403 on denial. For a non-model ability, route can middleware is another stated enforcement boundary. A hidden frontend control may reflect a choice, but it is not the server decision or its enforcement.",
103: "Draw the session identifier before successful authentication and the identifier used afterward. Regeneration belongs after Auth::attempt accepts credentials, preserves authenticated session data and changes the identifier. Invalidation instead removes data. Verifying the new identifier, rather than just a successful screen transition, checks the stated fixation-defense behavior.",
104: "Follow one consumer call into the options-store action. The action takes the input, awaits any request needed for confirmation and assigns this.property from the confirmed result. Other consumers reading that store property observe the update. This shared transition belongs in the action rather than being duplicated in each consumer. Keep the awaited result distinct from the proposed request input.",
105: "Trace the signed-out protected destination into a login redirect carrying to.fullPath. Excluding login from the redirect condition keeps the flow from redirecting back to itself. The guard's return determines cancel, redirect or continuation before navigation confirmation. Then mark server API protection separately: the client's navigation decision does not authorize backend requests.",
106: "Use the same post for owner and non-owner traces. The documented strict id comparison grants the owner update and denies the non-owner; Gate::authorize enforces the result before work. Verify the model-policy mapping follows discovery conventions or is explicitly registered. This separates policy selection from permission evaluation and prevents ‘authenticated’ from being mistaken for ‘owns this post’.",
107: "Follow the deployed request from SPA origin to cookie sending. Stateful domains classify the origin, session domain covers the subdomains, credentialed CORS approves access and the client includes credentials. For HTTPS, the secure-cookie setting restricts sending to HTTPS. These requirements complement rather than replace one another. A cookie's correct scope alone does not demonstrate that the actual production request carries it.",
108: "Trace successful and failed saves separately. Success awaits confirmation before replacing reactive record or list state, or re-fetches and replaces with confirmed data. Failure records feedback rather than applying the success update. A mapped list replacement should target the matching saved record. This makes the screen represent the server-confirmed outcome instead of assuming the edited draft is already final.",
110: "Arrange ownership before issuing requests. Fix the record's owner, then make the permitted owner, forbidden non-owner and anonymous cases distinct. ActingAs supplies the chosen authenticated identity; separate anonymous access verifies 401. AssertForbidden checks 403 for denial, and the owner case checks both specified success status and JSON. Explicit fixtures make those assertions exercise the intended different decisions.",
111: "Keep a compact evidence record for each smoke-check step: request, HTTP outcome and required public fields. Start at health, establish the disposable login session and exercise all CRUD operations on disposable records. Add direct nested-route or reload evidence for hosting. If a failure occurs, match server log context with that request before deciding which boundary was reached. This checks the deployed workflow rather than assembling unrelated passing observations.",
112: "Split failure recovery by received status. A later 401 or 419 after expiration may need login recovery, with requested destination preserved and the signed-out initial auth-store state restored where that options-store setup applies. A 403 selects forbidden feedback instead. Neither case should be parsed as success data. The status distinction prevents a forbidden action from repeatedly sending the user through login as though authentication alone could resolve it."
}
walkthroughs.update({
16: 'Trace two supplied module interfaces. In the named-export case, identify the exported binding helper and match import { helper } to it at the given module path. In the default-export case, the consumer uses no braces and may assign the default export a local name. The path identifies which module is read; braces and the name identify the export form expected there. Check all three before changing the helper implementation. This trace does not add a separate module-loading configuration rule: it explains the named-versus-default syntax supplied in the brief. A consumer choosing a local default-import name does not thereby create a named export of that name in the source file.',
18: 'Follow the supplied target settings from configuration to deployment values. Check the selected database connection and its host, port, database and credentials in the intended configuration. Then compare external environment values with .env, since external values can override that file. Review .env.example separately: it should provide placeholders, not target secrets. Finally confirm APP_DEBUG=false for public production error output. These are different checks even though they all concern environment preparation. A correct example template does not prove the target database settings are correct, and a correct database connection does not by itself settle whether public debug details are disabled.',
22: 'Trace a relevant failure message through the selected logging channel and its minimum severity. Compare that threshold with the supplied production requirement, then inspect which channels the stack combines. Public debug output remains disabled independently of that internal logging choice. Finally examine message content and any exposed logging events for sensitive information. This separates useful internal failure evidence from public error presentation rather than assuming either boundary protects the other automatically. A channel configuration should retain the failures the task needs without turning the log itself into a place for credentials or other sensitive data.',
44: 'Inspect the supplied public API address as a client-exposed build value. Its VITE_ name makes it available through import.meta.env under the default prefix rule; its value remains a string. If another supplied custom value looks like false or a number, do not assume its textual appearance changes that representation. Next ask whether each value is suitable for embedding in delivered client code. A public address fits this example, while a private key or password does not. This trace explains exposure and representation without introducing an extra conversion operation or pretending that an environment file makes client-embedded values secret.',
52: 'Compare a supplied body-and-status pair with the constructed response. The array provides JSON content, the explicit status supplies the outcome, and response()->json sets the JSON content type. Then check whether the contract also asks for headers carrying metadata outside the body. Those headers are a separate part of the response rather than an inference from an ordinary JSON property. Repeat the comparison for a different supplied outcome: changing the body alone does not select the required status. This is why the contract is reviewed at the body, status and header levels instead of saying a response is correct merely because its content is valid JSON.',
67: 'Trace a title failure into errors.title as a list of messages, keeping the overall message separate. For a nested supplied field, retain the stated dot-notation key. The JSON-expecting request receives 422, so the consumer can check the outcome and then read the structured feedback. If constructing a custom contract, preserve the specified field-to-message mapping and pair it with 422 through response()->json. This contrasts structured field errors with an undifferentiated error string without adding another response shape. The request rules trigger the normal failure; custom serialization is only for the explicitly supplied alternative contract.',
81: 'Compare the response shape before and after the supplied additive field change. Earlier public names and values should remain present, and the configured outer wrapper remains part of what the consumer accesses. assertJson with an expected fragment checks required earlier data while permitting additional properties. That is not a claim that every possible contract change is compatible. Separately record maintenance-mode availability during updates: Laravel disables the application and returns 503. Keeping these checks separate prevents an additive field test from being mistaken for a deployment-availability test, or maintenance behavior from being treated as a response-shape detail.',
95: 'Trace each participating test through isolated setup, a request and stored-result assertions. Persist fixture records with explicit factory overrides so the supplied case is controlled. After the operation, assertDatabaseHas checks for a matching row, while assertDatabaseMissing checks that a matching row is absent. RefreshDatabase prevents earlier participating state from interfering; when the schema is current it uses a transaction instead of migration for each test. This is a bounded statement about the documented isolation pattern, not a promise about all unrelated tests or databases. Stored-result assertions still check the operation itself rather than relying on isolation to imply that the expected write occurred.'
})
